    "title": "October 8 recap: Northwest Indiana news you may have missed today",
    "text": "October 8 recap: Northwest Indiana news you may have missed today\nThursday's news: What you missed while you were at work.\n(10) updates to this series since\nThe last of three defendants in the 2023 killing of 13-year-old Orie Dodson in Gary was sentenced Thursday.\nThe Michigan City Common Council recently approved a resolution calling for NIPSCO to strengthen staffing, reliability and storm response.\nMatt Kooy was sworn in as the Crown Point Fire Department's newest firefighter Thursday, while firefighter and paramedic Josh McBurney was promoted to lieutenant.\nA judge has ruled that Hammond Mayor Thomas McDermott Jr. did not defame Speros Batistatos by criticizing his work at the South Shore Convention and Visitors Authority.\nA crash involving several vehicles is disrupting Thursday afternoon traffic on the Borman Expressway. Here's what INDOT has reported so far.\nThe hospital served Hammond residents for more than a century before closing in 2022. Two companies have submitted competing bids, but city officials aren't revealing their redevelopment plans yet.\nA free Trunk-or-Treat event will bring Halloween festivities to Michigan City later this month.\nLaPorte County will host a one-stop shop for storm assistance this Friday and Saturday. Anyone impacted by the August storms can get help applying for individual assistance and more.\nWinter heating bills are headed higher again. NIPSCO forecasts a 1.1% increase, but actual costs will depend heavily on the weather.\nJust two weeks remain in the high school football regular season and The Times High School Football Podcast is back to catch you up on everything you've missed.",
    "summary": "(10) updates to this series since The last of three defendants in the 2023 killing of 13-year-old Orie Dodson in Gary was sentenced Thursday. The Michigan City Common Council recently approved a resolution calling for NIPSCO to strengthen staffing, reliability and storm response. A judge has ruled that Hammond Mayor Thomas McDermott Jr. did not defame Speros Batistatos by criticizing his work at the South Shore Convention and Visitors Authority. A crash involving several vehicles is disrupting Thursday afternoon traffic on the Borman Expressway. Two companies have submitted competing bids, but city officials aren't revealing their redevelopment plans yet.",
